        }
      ]
    },
    "ex-cg-wall-form-and-line-restrictions-of-a-plane-rotation": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set and let $m$ be a Coxeter matrix on $S$, so that $m(s,s)=1$ and $m(s,t)=m(t,s)\\in\\{2,3,\\dots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ for $s\\ne t$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]).\n\n**(1) The space and its distinguished functions.** Let $V:=\\mathbb R^S$ be the real vector space of all functions $S\\to\\mathbb R$, with pointwise operations ([[def-function-space]]); it is a vector space over the ordered field $\\mathbb R$ ([[thm-reals-ordered-field]]). Write $u(s)$ for the value of $u$ at $s$ and put $e_s(s):=1$, $e_s(t):=0$ for $t\\ne s$.\n\n**(2) The Coxeter form.** For finite $m(s,t)$ put $c(s,t):=\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))$ ([[def-sine-and-cosine-by-power-series]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]]) and for $m(s,t)=\\infty$ put $c(s,t):=1$; then $c(s,t)=c(t,s)$ and $c(s,s)=\\cos\\pi=-1$. The **Coxeter form** $B$ is the unique symmetric bilinear form on $V$ with $B(e_s,e_t)=-c(s,t)$ for all $s,t\\in S$; in particular $B(e_s,e_s)=1$ and $B(e_s,e_t)=-\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))$ for finite $m(s,t)$, while $B(e_s,e_t)=-1$ when $m(s,t)=\\infty$ ([[def-bilinear-symmetric-skew-and-alternating-forms]], [[thm-bilinear-forms-correspond-to-linear-maps-into-the-dual]]). **No positive definiteness, definiteness or nondegeneracy of $B$ is presumed**: $\\operatorname{rad}(B):=\\{u:B(u,v)=0\\text{ for all }v\\in V\\}$ may be nonzero ([[def-matrix-radicals-rank-and-nondegeneracy-of-a-bilinear-form]]), and the form may be indefinite ([[def-definiteness-inertia-and-signature-data-over-the-reals]]). A linear map $g:V\\to V$ is **$B$-preserving** when $B(gu,gw)=B(u,w)$ for all $u,w\\in V$.\n\n**(3) Reflections.** For $a\\in V$ with $B(a,a)\\ne0$ define the **reflection with normal $a$** by $$r_a(v):=v-\\frac{2B(v,a)}{B(a,a)}a .$$ The definition asserts neither that $r_a$ is linear or an involution nor that $\\ker B(-,a)=\\{v:B(v,a)=0\\}$ is a hyperplane; these are proved in [[lem-cg-reflection-form-invariance-and-rank-two-orders]]. When $B(a,a)=0$ the symbol $r_a$ is not defined.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "lem-cg-reflection-form-invariance-and-rank-two-orders",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$, $m$, $V=\\mathbb R^S$, $B$ and $r_a$ be as in [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]].\n\n**(1) Well-definedness of $B$.** The functions $e_s$ ($s\\in S$) form a basis of $V$, and there is exactly one symmetric bilinear form $B$ on $V$ with $B(e_s,e_t)=-\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t))$ for finite $m(s,t)$ and $B(e_s,e_t)=-1$ for $m(s,t)=\\infty$; it satisfies $B(u,w)=\\sum_{s,t\\in S}u(s)w(t)B(e_s,e_t)$ for all $u,w\\in V$.\n\n**(2) Reflection identities.** Let $a\\in V$ with $B(a,a)\\ne0$. Then $r_a$ is linear, $r_a^2=\\mathrm{id}_V$, $r_a(a)=-a$, $r_a(v)=v$ for every $v$ with $B(v,a)=0$, and $B(r_au,r_aw)=B(u,w)$ for all $u,w\\in V$. Moreover $\\ker B(-,a)$ is a linear subspace of dimension $\\dim V-1$ (a hyperplane of $V$, in the codimension-one sense) and is fixed pointwise by $r_a$.\n\n**(3) The rank-two plane.** Let $s\\ne t$ in $S$, put $P:=\\mathbb Re_s+\\mathbb Re_t$ and $c:=c(s,t)$ as in [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]] (so $c=1$ when $m(s,t)=\\infty$). Then:\n\n(i) $B|_P$ has Gram matrix $\\begin{pmatrix}1&-c\\\\-c&1\\end{pmatrix}$; for finite $m$ it is positive definite, with $B(x_se_s+x_te_t,\\,x_se_s+x_te_t)=(x_s-cx_t)^2+\\sin^2(\\pi/m)\\,x_t^2$; for $m=\\infty$ it is positive semidefinite, i.e. $B(x,x)\\ge0$ for all $x\\in P$, with radical $\\mathbb R(e_s+e_t)$.\n\n(ii) $r_s$ and $r_t$ fix every element of $P^\\perp=\\{v\\in V:B(v,e_s)=B(v,e_t)=0\\}$ pointwise; and if $m<\\infty$ then $V=P\\oplus P^\\perp$.\n\n(iii) The product $A:=r_sr_t$ has, in the ordered basis $(e_s,e_t)$ of $P$, the matrix $A=\\begin{pmatrix}4c^2-1&-2c\\\\2c&-1\\end{pmatrix}$, of determinant $1$.\n\n(iv) If $m<\\infty$ then $\\operatorname{tr}A=2\\cos(2\\pi/m)$, $A^m=\\mathrm{id}_V$, and $A^k\\ne\\mathrm{id}_V$ for $0<k<m$. If $m=\\infty$ then $A=\\mathrm{id}_P+N$ on $P$ for some $N$ with $N\\ne0$ and $N^2=0$; hence $(r_sr_t)^k|_P=\\mathrm{id}_P+kN$ for every $k\\in\\mathbb Z$, so $r_sr_t$ has infinite order on $V$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2",
            "2.1",
            "2.3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "thm-cg-finite-coxeter-classification-including-h-and-dihedral",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set with Coxeter matrix $m$ and diagram $\\Gamma$\n([[def-cg-coxeter-diagram-components-and-finite-type]]), $W$ the presented group\nwith length $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]),\n$V=\\mathbb R^S$ with Coxeter form $B$ and cosine matrix\n$C=(B(e_s,e_t))_{s,t\\in S}=(-\\cos(\\pi/m(s,t)))_{s,t}$\n([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]]).\n\n**(1) Irreducible case.** If $\\Gamma$ is connected, then $W$ is finite if and\nonly if $\\Gamma$ is isomorphic as a labelled graph\n([[def-graph-isomorphism-and-complement]]) to one of the following **standard\ndiagrams**:\n\n- $A_n$, $n\\ge1$: a path on $n$ vertices, all edges labelled $3$;\n- $B_n$, $n\\ge2$: a path on $n$ vertices whose labels are $3,\\dots,3,4$ (one\n  edge of label $4$, at an end);\n- $D_n$, $n\\ge4$: the star with one degree-$3$ vertex and three arms of\n  $1,1,n-3$ vertices, all edges labelled $3$;\n- $E_6$, $E_7$, $E_8$: the stars with arms of $1,2,2$; $1,2,3$; $1,2,4$ vertices,\n  all edges labelled $3$;\n- $F_4$: the path on four vertices with labels $3,4,3$;\n- $H_3$: the path on three vertices with labels $3,5$, and $H_4$: the path on\n  four vertices with labels $3,3,5$;\n- $I_2(m)$, $3\\le m<\\infty$: two vertices joined by a single edge labelled $m$.\n\n**(2) Reducible case.** For an arbitrary finite $S$, with components of $\\Gamma$\non the vertex sets $S_1,\\dots,S_k$, the group $W$ is finite if and only if every\ncomponent $\\Gamma_{S_i}$ is one of the diagrams of (1); in that case\n$W\\cong W_{S_1}\\times\\cdots\\times W_{S_k}$\n([[lem-cg-diagram-products-and-invariant-form-comparison]] (1),\n[[def-external-direct-product-of-groups]]). Thus the finite Coxeter systems are\nexactly the direct products of the irreducible types listed in (1).\n\n**(3) Positivity of the listed diagrams.** For every diagram $\\Gamma$ of the\nlist (1) the form $B$ is positive definite. In more detail: every proper\nprincipal submatrix of the matrix of $B$ is a block diagonal matrix whose blocks\nare matrices of listed diagrams of smaller rank, and the determinant of the full\n$n\\times n$ cosine matrix of each listed diagram, multiplied by $2^n$ (i.e.\n$\\det(2C)$), is\n$$A_n: n+1,\\quad B_n: 2,\\quad D_n: 4,\\quad E_6: 3,\\ E_7: 2,\\ E_8: 1,\\quad F_4: 1,\\quad H_3: 3-\\sqrt5,\\quad H_4: \\frac{7-3\\sqrt5}2,\\quad I_2(m): 4\\sin^2(\\pi/m),$$\nall of which are positive. Hence $B$ is positive definite for each listed\ndiagram (Sylvester's criterion;\n[[thm-sylvesters-criterion-for-positive-definiteness]]), and by (1) of\n[[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]] each such diagram defines a\nfinite Coxeter group.\n\n**(4) Coincidences.** As Coxeter systems one has $A_2=I_2(3)$,\n$B_2=C_2=I_2(4)$ and $G_2=I_2(6)$; more generally with the usual naming\nconventions $A_1=B_1$ (the one-vertex diagram), $A_3=D_3$ (the path on three\nvertices), $H_2=I_2(5)$, and $A_1\\times A_1=I_2(2)$ if the notation $I_2(m)$ is\nextended to $m=2$, the disconnected two-vertex diagram (no edge, since $m=2$\ndraws no edge). Apart from these identifications the diagrams of (1) are\npairwise non-isomorphic, and the classification list is therefore the\nduplicate-free list $A_n$ ($n\\ge1$), $B_n$ ($n\\ge2$), $D_n$ ($n\\ge4$),\n$E_6,E_7,E_8,F_4,H_3,H_4$ and $I_2(m)$ with $m\\ge3$, $m\\notin\\{3,4\\}$,\nwhere $I_2(3),I_2(4)$ are also written $A_2,B_2$ and $I_2(5),I_2(6)$ are also\nwritten $H_2,G_2$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-cg-coxeter-diagram-components-and-finite-type",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set and let $m:S\\times S\\to\\{1,2,3,\\dots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$ be a\nCoxeter matrix on $S$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), with\npresented group $W$, length function $\\ell$ and standard parabolics\n$W_T=\\langle s:s\\in T\\rangle$ for $T\\subseteq S$ ([[def-generated-subgroup]],\n[[def-group]]).\n\n**(1) The diagram.** The **Coxeter diagram** $\\Gamma(W,S)$ of $(W,S)$ has vertex\nset $S$, and two distinct vertices $s\\ne t$ are joined by an edge exactly when\n$m(s,t)\\ge3$; that edge carries the **label**\n$m(s,t)\\in\\{3,4,5,\\dots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$. By convention the label $3$ is omitted\n(an unlabelled edge has label $3$) and no edge is drawn when $m(s,t)=2$. Thus\n$\\Gamma$ is a finite simple graph whose edges are labelled in\n$\\{3,4,\\dots\\}\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$\n([[def-graph-adjacency-incidence-neighbourhood-and-degree]]), and $m$ is\nrecovered from the pair $(S,\\Gamma)$ by $m(s,s)=1$; $m(s,t)=m(t,s)=2$ if\n$\\{s,t\\}$ is not an edge; $m(s,t)=3$ if $\\{s,t\\}$ is an unlabelled edge; and\n$m(s,t)=$ the label otherwise. In particular $m\\mapsto\\Gamma$ is injective on\nthe Coxeter matrices on $S$. For $T\\subseteq S$ the **subdiagram** $\\Gamma_T$ is\nthe induced labelled graph on $T$, i.e. the diagram of the restricted matrix\n$m|_{T\\times T}$.\n\n**(2) Graph-theoretic vocabulary.** A **cycle** of $\\Gamma$ is a cycle of the\nunderlying simple graph ([[def-graph-walk-trail-path-and-cycle]]); a **path** (or\n**chain**) is a diagram whose underlying graph is a path. $\\Gamma$ is\n**connected** when its underlying graph is connected\n([[def-connected-graph-and-connected-component]]); its **components** are the\nconnected components of the underlying graph, and their vertex sets are\nnonempty and partition $S$. For $s\\in S$ the **neighbours** of $s$ are\n$N(s):=\\{t\\in S:t\\ne s,\\ m(s,t)\\ge3\\}$ and the **degree** of $s$ is $|N(s)|$\n([[def-graph-adjacency-incidence-neighbourhood-and-degree]]).\n\n**(3) Irreducibility.** $(W,S)$ is **irreducible** when $\\Gamma$ is connected,\nand **reducible** otherwise. (For $S=\\emptyset$ the diagram is empty; the\ntrivial system is not called irreducible.)\n\n**(4) Finite type.** $(W,S)$ - and, by extension, $\\Gamma$ - is **of finite\ntype**, or **spherical**, when the group $W$ is finite. This is a property of\n$W$ itself: **no list of diagrams is part of the definition**, and no\npositivity, definiteness or nondegeneracy of any bilinear form, and no geometric\nrealization, is asserted here.\n\n**(5) Invariance and abstentions.** By the conventions of\n[[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], $m(s,t)$ is the order of $st$\nin $W$; consequently an isomorphism of Coxeter systems $(W,S)\\to(W',S')$ (a\ngroup isomorphism carrying $S$ onto $S'$) matches the two diagrams, so\nconnectedness, irreducibility and finite type depend only on the isomorphism\ntype of $(W,S)$. It is **not** asserted here that $W$ is the direct product of\nthe standard parabolics of its components - that is the content of\n[[lem-cg-diagram-products-and-invariant-form-comparison]] - nor that a diagram\nof finite type is one of the diagrams classified in\n[[thm-cg-finite-coxeter-classification-including-h-and-dihedral]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $S$, $m$, $V$, $B$ be as in [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], let $W$ be the presented Coxeter group of [[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]] with its universal property, and put $r_s:=r_{e_s}$ for $s\\in S$ ([[lem-cg-reflection-form-invariance-and-rank-two-orders]]).\n\n**(1)** Write $\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ for the group of invertible linear maps $V\\to V$, the unit group of the monoid of linear endomorphisms under composition ([[def-linear-isomorphism-and-invertible-linear-map]], [[def-vector-space-of-linear-maps]], [[lem-composition-and-identity-linear-maps]], [[lem-monoid-units-form-a-group]]). The **canonical reflection homomorphism** is the group homomorphism $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ ([[def-group-homomorphism]]) that satisfies $\\rho(s)=r_s$ for every $s\\in S$; its existence and uniqueness are established by [[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]], which is this definition's recorded justifier. For $w\\in W$ and $v\\in V$ write $\\rho(w)v$ for the image.\n\n**(2)** The **root system** of the pair is $\\Phi:=\\{\\rho(w)e_s:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}\\subset V$; its elements are the **roots**. The set of **reflections** of $W$ is $T:=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}\\subset W$; that $\\rho(wsw^{-1})$ is the reflection $r_{\\rho(w)e_s}$ and that every root is $B$-non-isotropic are proved in [[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]].\n\n**(3)** The **positive cone** is $V_+:=\\{\\sum_{s\\in S}\\lambda_se_s:\\lambda_s\\in\\mathbb R,\\ \\lambda_s\\ge0\\}$ and the **negative cone** is $-V_+$ ([[def-linear-combination-and-span]]).\n\nNo positivity of $\\rho(w)e_s$ for fixed $w$, no faithfulness or injectivity of $\\rho$, no discreteness and no nondegeneracy of $B$ is asserted by this definition.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-cg-orthogonal-wall-form-and-subspace-restriction",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(V,B)$ be a finite-dimensional real inner product space ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]); on this page $V=\\mathbb R^S$ with the positive definite Coxeter form $B$ of a Coxeter system of finite type ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]]), and $\\mathrm O(V)$ is the group of $B$-preserving invertible linear maps ([[def-linear-isometry-and-orthogonal-or-unitary-operator]]). Let $M$, $F$ and $\\le_{\\mathrm O}$ be as in [[def-cg-reflection-length-absolute-order-and-moved-space]]. Then:\n\n**(1) Basic identities.** For every $A\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ one has $F(A)=F(A^{-1})$, $M(A)=M(A^{-1})$, $M(A)=F(A)^\\perp$, $V=M(A)\\oplus F(A)$, $A(M(A))=M(A)$, $A(F(A))=F(A)$, and $(A-\\mathrm{id})|_{M(A)}:M(A)\\to M(A)$ is a bijection ([[cor-double-orthogonal-complement-and-dimension]], [[thm-rank-nullity]]). For all $X,Y\\in\\mathrm O(V)$, $M(XY)\\subseteq M(X)+X\\,M(Y)$ and hence $\\dim M(XY)\\le\\dim M(X)+\\dim M(Y)$.\n\n**(2) The Wall form.** For $A\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ put\n\n$$\\chi_A(u,v):=B\\bigl((A-\\mathrm{id})|_{M(A)}^{-1}u,\\ v\\bigr)\\qquad(u,v\\in M(A)).$$\n\nThen $\\chi_A$ is a bilinear form on $M(A)$ satisfying\n\n$$\\chi_A(u,v)+\\chi_A(v,u)=-B(u,v)\\qquad(u,v\\in M(A));$$\n\nin particular $\\chi_A$ is nondegenerate and its symmetric part is $-\\tfrac12B|_{M(A)}$ ([[def-adjoint-of-a-linear-map-between-inner-product-spaces]], [[prop-adjoint-algebra]]).\n\n**(3) Subspace restriction.** Let $A\\in\\mathrm O(V)$, let $U\\subseteq M(A)$ be a subspace and let $\\Pi_U:V\\to U$ be the orthogonal projection ([[def-orthogonal-projection]], [[thm-finite-dimensional-orthogonal-decomposition]]). There is a unique $H_U\\in\\operatorname{End}(U)$ with $B(H_Uu,v)=\\chi_A(u,v)$ for all $u,v\\in U$, and it satisfies $H_U+H_U^*=-\\mathrm{id}_U$, so that $H_U$ is invertible. Define $A_U\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ by $A_Uu=u+H_U^{-1}u$ for $u\\in U$ and $A_U=\\mathrm{id}$ on $U^\\perp$. Then $M(A_U)=U$ and\n\n$$(A_U-\\mathrm{id})|_U^{-1}=H_U,\\qquad H_Uu=\\Pi_U\\bigl((A-\\mathrm{id})|_{M(A)}\\bigr)^{-1}u\\ \\ (u\\in U).$$\n\nAn element $A\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ is a reflection (that is, $\\dim M(A)=1$) if and only if $A=\\mathrm{id}-2\\Pi_L$ for the line $L=M(A)$; in particular every line $L\\subseteq V$ is the moved space of exactly one reflection of $\\mathrm O(V)$ ([[thm-finite-dimensional-isometry-characterisations]]).\n\n**(4) The restriction theorem.** For every subspace $U\\subseteq M(A)$ one has $A_U\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$, that is $\\dim M(A)=\\dim U+\\dim M(A_U^{-1}A)$; and for $U\\subseteq U'\\subseteq M(A)$ one has $(A_{U'})_U=A_U$, hence $A_U\\le_{\\mathrm O}A_{U'}$. Conversely every $B\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ with $B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$ satisfies $M(B)\\subseteq M(A)$, $\\chi_A|_{M(B)}=\\chi_B$ and $B=A_{M(B)}$. Consequently the assignment $U\\mapsto A_U$ is a bijection from the set of subspaces of $M(A)$ onto $\\{B\\in\\mathrm O(V):B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A\\}$, with inverse $B\\mapsto M(B)$, and it is an order isomorphism for inclusion of subspaces and $\\le_{\\mathrm O}$.\n\n**(5) Rank-length equality and prefixes.** Let $A\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ and let $r_1,\\dots,r_k\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ be reflections with $A=r_1r_2\\cdots r_k$. Then $k\\ge\\dim M(A)$, and $A$ is a product of exactly $\\dim M(A)$ reflections. Moreover $B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$ if and only if there are a shortest factorization $A=r_1\\cdots r_m$ (so $m=\\dim M(A)$) and an index $k$ with $B=r_1\\cdots r_k$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2",
            "2.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "thm-cg-carter-reflection-length-and-absolute-order",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system of finite type with $S$ finite, with $V=\\mathbb R^S$, positive definite Coxeter form $B$, canonical reflection representation $\\rho$, root system $\\Phi$, reflection set $T$ and length function $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], [[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], [[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]]), and let $\\ell_T$, $M$, $F$ and $\\le_T$ be as in [[def-cg-reflection-length-absolute-order-and-moved-space]]. Then:\n\n**(1) Carter's formula.** For every $w\\in W$,\n\n$$\\ell_T(w)=\\dim M(w)=\\dim V-\\dim F(w).$$\n\n**(2) The absolute order.** $\\le_T$ is a partial order on $W$ ([[def-partial-order]]), and:\n\n**(i)** $u\\le_T v$ holds if and only if there are reflections $t_1,\\dots,t_m\\in T$ and an index $k\\le m$ such that $u=t_1\\cdots t_k$ and $v=t_1\\cdots t_m$ are shortest reflection factorizations, that is, $k=\\ell_T(u)$ and $m=\\ell_T(v)$ (a shortest reflection factorization of $u$ is a prefix of one of $v$);\n\n**(ii)** $\\ell_T(1)=0$, $u<_T v$ implies $\\ell_T(u)<\\ell_T(v)$, and $\\ell_T(y)=\\ell_T(x)+1$ whenever $y$ covers $x$; hence $\\ell_T$ is a rank function and every interval $[u,v]_T=\\{x\\in W:u\\le_Tx\\le_Tv\\}$ is finite ([[def-graded-poset-and-rank]]);\n\n**(iii)** $|\\ell_T(u)-\\ell_T(v)|\\le\\ell_T(u^{-1}v)$, $\\ell_T(u^{-1})=\\ell_T(u)$ and $\\ell_T(vuv^{-1})=\\ell_T(u)$ for all $u,v\\in W$;\n\n**(iv)** $u\\le_T v$ implies $M(u)\\subseteq M(v)$ and $F(v)\\subseteq F(u)$.\n\n**(3) Moved-space rigidity under a common upper bound.** Let $\\alpha,\\beta,\\delta\\in W$ with $\\alpha\\le_T\\delta$ and $\\beta\\le_T\\delta$. Then\n\n$$\\alpha\\le_T\\beta\\iff M(\\alpha)\\subseteq M(\\beta);$$\n\nin particular $M(\\alpha)=M(\\beta)$ implies $\\alpha=\\beta$, and $u\\mapsto M(u)$ is an order isomorphism from $[1,\\delta]_T$ onto its image ordered by inclusion. The proof of the converse uses the common upper bound $\\delta$, through the restriction of $\\delta$ to the subspace $M(\\alpha)$; the converse is claimed only under this hypothesis (see the companion example of the $I_2(4)$ rotation, where the hypothesis fails).",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "lem-cg-reflection-factorizations-and-independent-normals",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system of finite type with $S$ finite, with $V=\\mathbb R^S$, positive definite Coxeter form $B$, canonical reflection representation $\\rho$, root system $\\Phi$ and reflection set $T$ ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], [[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]]), with the chamber system $wC$, the open faces $wC_I$ and the root hyperplanes $H_\\alpha=\\{x\\in V:B(x,\\alpha)=0\\}$ of the transferred dual action ([[def-cg-finite-reflection-arrangement-and-spherical-chambers]]), and let $\\ell_T$, $M$, $F$ and $\\le_{\\mathrm O}$ be as in [[def-cg-reflection-length-absolute-order-and-moved-space]]; for $u\\in W$ write $M(u)=M(\\rho(u))$ and $F(u)=F(\\rho(u))$. Then:\n\n**(1) Root normals in the moved space.** Let $w\\in W$ with $w\\ne1$. Then $\\dim M(w)>0$ and there is a root $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ with $F(w)\\subseteq H_\\alpha$. For every such root one has $\\alpha\\in M(w)$, and the reflection $t_\\alpha\\in T$ with $\\rho(t_\\alpha)=r_\\alpha$ ([[thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange]] (1), [[lem-cg-reflection-form-invariance-and-rank-two-orders]] (2)) satisfies\n\n$$\\rho(t_\\alpha)\\le_{\\mathrm O}\\rho(w),\\qquad \\dim M(w)=1+\\dim M(t_\\alpha w).$$\n\n**(2) Factorizations and Carter's formula.** Every $w\\in W$ is a product $w=t_1t_2\\cdots t_k$ of $k=\\dim M(w)$ reflections $t_i\\in T$, and no product of fewer elements of $T$ represents $w$; equivalently\n\n$$\\ell_T(w)=\\dim M(w).$$\n\n**(3) Independent normals.** Let $t_1,\\dots,t_m\\in T$ and let $\\alpha_i\\in\\Phi$ be the root with $\\rho(t_i)=r_{\\alpha_i}$. Then $\\dim M(t_1\\cdots t_m)\\le m$; and if $\\dim M(t_1\\cdots t_m)=m$ — in particular if $t_1\\cdots t_m$ is a shortest reflection factorization of its product — then the $m$ vectors\n\n$$\\alpha_1,\\ \\rho(t_1)\\alpha_2,\\ \\rho(t_1t_2)\\alpha_3,\\ \\dots,\\ \\rho(t_1t_2\\cdots t_{m-1})\\alpha_m$$\n\nare linearly independent ([[def-linear-independence]]) and span $M(t_1\\cdots t_m)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-cg-root-inversion-formulas-and-strong-exchange",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, $m$ a Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group with length $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), $V=\\mathbb R^S$ with Coxeter form $B$, canonical reflection homomorphism $\\rho$, root system $\\Phi=\\Phi_+\\sqcup\\Phi_-$ and reflection set $T=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]], [[thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity]]); every root has $B$-norm one.\n\n**(1) The root-reflection dictionary.** For $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$ choose $w\\in W$, $s\\in S$ with $\\alpha=\\rho(w)e_s$ and put $t_\\alpha:=wsw^{-1}\\in T$. Then:\n(i) $t_\\alpha$ is independent of the chosen representation of $\\alpha$;\n(ii) $\\rho(t_\\alpha)=r_\\alpha$, the reflection with normal $\\alpha$; and $t_{\\rho(w)\\alpha}=w\\,t_\\alpha\\,w^{-1}$ for all $w\\in W$, $\\alpha\\in\\Phi$;\n(iii) $t_{-\\alpha}=t_\\alpha$, and for $\\alpha,\\beta\\in\\Phi$ one has $t_\\alpha=t_\\beta$ if and only if $\\alpha=\\pm\\beta$;\n(iv) the induced map $\\{\\pm\\alpha:\\alpha\\in\\Phi\\}\\to T$ is a bijection; so is the map $\\Phi_+\\to T$, $\\alpha\\mapsto t_\\alpha$.\n\n**(2) Inversion formula.** For every $w\\in W$ one has $|N(w)|=\\ell(w)$ (with $N$ as in [[def-cg-geometric-inversion-set]]); and for every reduced expression $w=s_1\\cdots s_n$,\n$$N(w)=\\bigl\\{\\rho(s_{i+1}\\cdots s_n)^{-1}e_{s_i}:1\\le i\\le n\\bigr\\},\\qquad N(w^{-1})=\\bigl\\{\\rho(s_1\\cdots s_{i-1})e_{s_i}:1\\le i\\le n\\bigr\\},$$\nthe displayed elements being pairwise distinct positive roots.\n\n**(3) Strong exchange.** Let $w\\in W$ and $t\\in T$ satisfy $\\ell(tw)<\\ell(w)$, and let $w=s_1\\cdots s_n$ be a reduced expression. Then there is a unique $i\\in\\{1,\\dots,n\\}$ with\n$$tw=s_1\\cdots\\widehat{s_i}\\cdots s_n,\\qquad t=r_i:=s_1\\cdots s_{i-1}s_is_{i-1}\\cdots s_1;$$\nmoreover, if $\\alpha\\in\\Phi_+$ is the positive root with $t=t_\\alpha$, then $\\alpha\\in N(w^{-1})$ and $\\alpha=\\rho(s_1\\cdots s_{i-1})e_{s_i}$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F5",
          "source": "thm-cg-root-length-criterion-and-faithfulness",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $S$ be a finite set, $m$ a Coxeter matrix, $W$ the presented group with length function $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), $V=\\mathbb R^S$ with Coxeter form $B$ and canonical reflection homomorphism $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ with root system $\\Phi$ ([[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]), and let $\\Phi_+,\\Phi_-$ be the positive and negative roots and $C^\\circ$ the open chamber of the dual action ([[thm-cg-root-sign-and-simple-reflection-positivity]]).\n\n**(1) Root-length criterion.** For all $w\\in W$ and $s\\in S$,\n$$\\ell(ws)>\\ell(w)\\iff\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_+,\\qquad \\ell(ws)<\\ell(w)\\iff\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-.$$\n\n**(2) Disjoint chambers.** If $wC^\\circ\\cap C^\\circ\\ne\\emptyset$ for some $w\\in W$, then $w=1$. Equivalently, the chambers $wC^\\circ$ ($w\\in W$) are pairwise disjoint, that is, $C^\\circ$ is prefundamental for the $W$-action on $V^*$ in the sense that $wC^\\circ\\cap C^\\circ\\ne\\emptyset$ forces $w=1$ for every $w\\in W$.\n\n**(3) Faithfulness.** The homomorphism $\\rho$ is injective, the dual action $W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V^*)$ is injective, and for every $w\\ne1$ there is $s\\in S$ with $\\rho(w)e_s\\in\\Phi_-$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1",
            "2.3",
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $\\gamma$ be the unique smallest positive zero of cosine supplied by [[thm-cosine-has-a-smallest-positive-zero]]. Define\n$$\\pi:=2\\gamma.$$\n\nThus $\\pi>0$ and $\\pi/2=\\gamma$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "lem-sine-positive-and-cosine-decreasing-on-zero-two",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For $0<x\\le2$, $\\sin x\\ge x-x^3/6\\ge x/3>0$. Also $\\cos2\\le-1/3$. Consequently $\\cos$ is strictly decreasing on $[0,2]$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(-x)=-\\sin x,\\qquad\\cos(-x)=\\cos x,\\qquad\\sin^2x+\\cos^2x=1.$$\nConsequently $|\\sin x|\\le1$ and $|\\cos x|\\le1$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "thm-double-angle-and-power-reduction-identities",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For every real $x$,\n$$\\sin(2x)=2\\sin x\\cos x,\\quad \\cos(2x)=\\cos^2x-\\sin^2x=2\\cos^2x-1=1-2\\sin^2x,$$\nand\n$$\\sin^2x=(1-\\cos2x)/2,\\qquad \\cos^2x=(1+\\cos2x)/2.$$\nWhen defined, $\\tan(2x)=2\\tan x/(1-\\tan^2x)$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-sine-and-cosine-addition-formulas]], [[cor-trigonometric-parity-and-pythagorean-identity]], [[def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "cor-complex-exponential-cartesian-form-modulus-and-eulers-identity",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For real $x,y$, $\\exp(x+iy)=e^x(\\cos y+i\\sin y)$ and $|\\exp(x+iy)|=e^x$. In particular $e^{i\\pi}+1=0$. The conventions and prerequisite facts used below are recorded in [[thm-complex-exponential-addition-and-real-extension]], [[thm-eulers-formula]], [[lem-complex-conjugation-and-modulus-laws]], [[thm-pythagorean-and-parity-identities-for-all-six-trigonometric-functions]], [[cor-exponential-reciprocal-and-positivity]], [[thm-quarter-turn-values-and-shift-formulas]], [[def-pi-via-first-positive-cosine-zero]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F6",
          "source": "def-tangent-cotangent-secant-cosecant",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Define\n$$\\tan x:=\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\\quad(x\\ne\\pi/2+m\\pi),\\qquad\\cot x:=\\frac{\\cos x}{\\sin x}\\quad(x\\ne m\\pi),$$\n$$\\sec x:=\\frac1{\\cos x}\\quad(x\\ne\\pi/2+m\\pi),\\qquad\\csc x:=\\frac1{\\sin x}\\quad(x\\ne m\\pi),$$\nwhere $m$ ranges over the integers. The exclusions are exactly the zero sets of [[thm-sine-cosine-zero-sets-and-fundamental-period]].",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F7",
          "source": "def-cg-reflection-length-absolute-order-and-moved-space",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Let $(W,S)$ be a Coxeter system of finite type with $S$ finite and length function $\\ell$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]], [[def-cg-coxeter-diagram-components-and-finite-type]]), with canonical reflection representation $\\rho:W\\to\\mathrm{GL}(V)$ on $V=\\mathbb R^S$, Coxeter form $B$, root system $\\Phi$ and reflection set $T=\\{wsw^{-1}:w\\in W,\\ s\\in S\\}$ ([[def-cg-real-coxeter-form-and-reflection]], [[def-cg-canonical-reflection-homomorphism]]). Since $W$ is finite, $B$ is positive definite ([[thm-cg-finite-type-positive-definite-criterion]]), so $(V,B)$ is a real inner product space ([[def-real-and-complex-inner-product-space]]), and $\\rho(w)$ preserves $B$ for every $w\\in W$ ([[lem-cg-reflection-representation-descends-and-root-norms]] (2)). Write $\\mathrm O(V)$ for the group of $B$-preserving invertible linear maps $V\\to V$ ([[def-linear-isometry-and-orthogonal-or-unitary-operator]]).\n\n**(1) Reflection length.** For $w\\in W$ put\n\n$$\\ell_T(w):=\\min\\{k\\in\\mathbb N:\\ \\text{there are }t_1,\\dots,t_k\\in T\\text{ with }w=t_1t_2\\cdots t_k\\},$$\n\nwhere the empty product ($k=0$) is the identity. The minimum exists because $S\\subseteq T$ and $S$ generates $W$ ([[def-hh-coxeter-matrix-word-group-and-length]]), so the admitted $k$ form a nonempty subset of $\\mathbb N$, which has a least element ([[thm-well-ordering-principle]]).\n\n**(2) Absolute order.** For $u,v\\in W$ define $u\\le_T v$ if and only if\n\n$$\\ell_T(v)=\\ell_T(u)+\\ell_T(u^{-1}v).$$\n\n**(3) Moved and fixed spaces.** For a linear map $A:V\\to V$ ([[def-linear-map]]) define the **moved space** and the **fixed space**\n\n$$M(A):=\\operatorname{im}(A-\\mathrm{id}_V),\\qquad F(A):=\\ker(A-\\mathrm{id}_V)$$\n\n([[def-kernel-and-image-of-a-linear-map]]). For $A,B\\in\\mathrm O(V)$ define the relation $B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$ if and only if\n\n$$\\dim M(A)=\\dim M(B)+\\dim M(B^{-1}A).$$\n\n**(4) Conventions and abstentions.** For $u\\in W$ write $M(u):=M(\\rho(u))$ and $F(u):=F(\\rho(u))$. This definition asserts no property of $\\le_T$ and $\\le_{\\mathrm O}$ beyond the displayed formulas: it asserts neither that either relation is a partial order, nor that $\\ell_T(w)=\\dim M(w)$, nor that $B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$ means that a shortest reflection factorization of $B$ is a prefix of one of $A$. Those properties are proved in [[thm-cg-carter-reflection-length-and-absolute-order]], the recorded justifier of this definition, and by the restriction and factorization lemmas of this page. No Choice is used: $S$, $W$, $\\Phi$ and $T$ are finite and every object is finite-dimensional or set-theoretic.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "By [F1] the matrix of $A$ in the basis $(e_s,e_t)$ has determinant $1$ and trace $2\\cos(2\\pi/m)$, and $A$ has order $m$; the trace differs from $\\operatorname{tr}\\mathrm{id}=2$ because $1-\\cos(2\\pi/m)=2\\sin^2(\\pi/m)>0$ by [F6], as $\\pi/m\\in(0,\\pi/3]\\subset(0,2]$ gives $\\sin(\\pi/m)>0$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F2",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-1-2",
          "claim": "Put $u:=(e_s+e_t)/\\sqrt{2-2c}$ and $\\tilde w:=(e_t-e_s)/\\sqrt{2+2c}$; the square roots are nonzero because $2-2c=4\\sin^2(\\pi/(2m))>0$ and $2+2c=4\\cos^2(\\pi/(2m))>0$, since $0<\\pi/(2m)\\le\\pi/6<2$ and $\\cos$ has no zero in $[0,\\pi/2)$ by [F6]. A direct computation with [F1] gives $B(u,u)=B(\\tilde w,\\tilde w)=1$ and $B(u,\\tilde w)=0$, so $(u,\\tilde w)$ is an oriented orthonormal basis, and computing $Au=\\cos\\theta\\,u+\\sin\\theta\\,\\tilde w$, $A\\tilde w=-\\sin\\theta\\,u+\\cos\\theta\\,\\tilde w$ with $\\theta:=2\\pi/m$: indeed $B(Au,u)=2c^2-1=\\cos\\theta$, $B(Au,\\tilde w)=2c\\sin(\\pi/m)=\\sin\\theta$, $B(A\\tilde w,u)=-\\sin\\theta$ and $B(A\\tilde w,\\tilde w)=2c^2-1=\\cos\\theta$, using $1-c^2=\\sin^2(\\pi/m)$ and the double-angle identities of [F6]. Hence in these coordinates $A$ is the rotation by $\\theta=2\\pi/m$, and $1$ is not an eigenvalue of $A$: the matrix of $A-\\mathrm{id}$ has determinant $(\\cos\\theta-1)^2+\\sin^2\\theta=2-2\\cos\\theta=4\\sin^2(\\theta/2)\\ne0$, since $\\theta/2=\\pi/m\\in(0,2]$. Therefore $F(A)=\\ker(A-\\mathrm{id})=0$ and $M(A)=F(A)^\\perp=V$ by [F3](1). Moreover $S_A=(A-\\mathrm{id})^{-1}$, computed from the displayed rotation matrix, is $\\tfrac12\\begin{pmatrix}-1&\\sin\\theta/(1-\\cos\\theta)\\\\-\\sin\\theta/(1-\\cos\\theta)&-1\\end{pmatrix}=-\\tfrac12\\mathrm{id}-\\tfrac12\\cot(\\theta/2)J$ with $J=\\begin{pmatrix}0&-1\\\\1&0\\end{pmatrix}$, because $\\sin\\theta/(1-\\cos\\theta)=\\cot(\\theta/2)$ by the double-angle identities; under the identification of the oriented plane with $\\mathbb C$, the matrix $J$ is multiplication by $i$, so $S_A$ is multiplication by $-\\tfrac12-\\tfrac i2\\cot(\\theta/2)$, and $(e^{i\\theta}-1)\\bigl(-\\tfrac12-\\tfrac i2\\cot(\\theta/2)\\bigr)=1$ by the displayed identity $e^{i\\theta}=\\cos\\theta+i\\sin\\theta$ together with $\\sin^2\\theta+\\cos^2\\theta=1$ and $\\sin\\theta/(1-\\cos\\theta)=\\cot(\\theta/2)$, so $S_A$ is multiplication by $1/(e^{i\\theta}-1)$. The identity $\\chi_A(u,v)+\\chi_A(v,u)=-B(u,v)$ with symmetric part $-\\tfrac12B$ is [F3](2).",
          "step": "1.2",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "F3",
            "F6"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "Part (iii). Take $m=4$, so $\\theta=\\pi/2$ and the rotation matrix of step 1.2 gives $A^2=\\begin{pmatrix}-1&0\\\\0&-1\\end{pmatrix}=-\\mathrm{id}_V$; hence $M(w_0)=\\operatorname{im}(-2\\mathrm{id})=V=M(A)$ for $w_0:=A^2$, so $M(w_0)\\subseteq M(A)$. By [F4] and step 1.2, $\\ell_T(A)=\\dim M(A)=2$ and $\\ell_T(w_0)=\\dim M(w_0)=2$; also $w_0^{-1}A=A^{-2}A=A^{-1}$ and $A^{-1}w_0=A^{-1}A^2=A$, and $A^{-1}=A^3$ has moved space $V$ because $F(A^{-1})=F(A)=0$, so $\\ell_T(w_0^{-1}A)=\\ell_T(A^{-1}w_0)=\\ell_T(A)=2$. Hence $\\ell_T(A)=2\\ne4=\\ell_T(w_0)+\\ell_T(w_0^{-1}A)$ and $\\ell_T(w_0)=2\\ne4=\\ell_T(A)+\\ell_T(A^{-1}w_0)$, so $w_0\\not\\le_TA$ and $A\\not\\le_Tw_0$ by [F7]. If some $\\delta\\in W$ satisfied $A\\le_T\\delta$ and $w_0\\le_T\\delta$, then $2=\\ell_T(A)\\le\\ell_T(\\delta)$ and $\\ell_T(\\delta)=\\dim M(\\delta)\\le\\dim V=2$ by [F4], so [F7] gives $\\ell_T(\\delta)=\\ell_T(A)+\\ell_T(A^{-1}\\delta)=2$ and hence $\\ell_T(A^{-1}\\delta)=0$; then $A^{-1}\\delta=1$, since $\\ell_T(x)=\\dim M(x)=0$ forces $\\rho(x)=\\mathrm{id}$ and $x=1$ by [F5], so $\\delta=A$, contradicting $w_0\\not\\le_TA$; thus $A$ and $w_0$ have no common upper bound. Finally $w_0=A^2$ is the longest element of $W=I_2(4)$: since $sA=(s)(st)=t$ and $A^{-1}=ts$, one has $sAs^{-1}=(sA)s=ts=A^{-1}$, so every element of $W=\\langle s,A\\rangle$ has the form $A^k$ or $A^ks$ with $0\\le k\\le3$, and the eight listed elements are distinct because $\\rho(A^ks)$ has a one-dimensional moved space while $\\rho(A^k)$ has moved space $V$ for $k=1,2,3$ and $0$ for $k=0$, and $A$ has order $4$ by [F1]; a word of length at most three in $s,t$ reduces to one of $1,s,t,st,ts,sts,tst$, and $A^2=stst$ is none of these (it is a rotation while $s,t,sts,tst$ are reflections, it is neither $1$ nor $A$ nor $A^3=A^{-1}=ts$ nor $st$ because $A$ has order $4$, and $A^2=st$ or $A^2=ts$ would force $A=1$ or $A^3=1$), while $A^2=stst$ is a word of length $4$; hence $\\ell(w_0)=4$ and $w_0$ is the unique element of maximal length.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "F1",
            "F4",
            "F5",
            "F7"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-2",
          "claim": "Part (ii), first half. Let $L\\subseteq V$ be a line; since $M(A)=V$ by step 1.2, $L\\subseteq M(A)$, so the operator $H_L=\\Pi_LS_A\\Pi_L$ of [F3](3) is defined. As $L$ is one-dimensional, $H_L$ is multiplication by a real scalar $\\lambda$, and the identity $H_L+H_L^*=-\\mathrm{id}_L$ of [F3](3) gives $2\\lambda=-1$, so $\\lambda=-\\tfrac12$; hence $A_L=\\mathrm{id}+H_L^{-1}$ acts on $L$ as $-\\mathrm{id}_L$ and on $L^\\perp$ as the identity, that is $A_L=\\mathrm{id}-2\\Pi_L$. By [F3](3) $M(A_L)=L$, and $A_L\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$ by [F3](4). The assignment $L\\mapsto A_L$ is a bijection from the lines of $V$ onto the reflections $B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$: [F3](4) gives a bijection $U\\mapsto A_U$ from the subspaces of $M(A)=V$ onto $\\{B:B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A\\}$ whose inverse is $B\\mapsto M(B)$ and which satisfies $M(A_U)=U$, and the one-dimensional subspaces of $V$ are the lines, while the elements $B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$ with $\\dim M(B)=1$ are exactly the reflections $B\\le_{\\mathrm O}A$ (the elements $B=\\mathrm{id}$ and $B=A$ correspond to $U=0$ and $U=V$ and are not reflections, as $\\dim M(A)=2$).",
          "step": "2.2",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "F3"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-3",
          "claim": "$T$ has exactly $m$ elements. Since $A=st$ one has $sA=t$ and $A^{-1}=ts$, and the set $W_0:=\\{A^j,A^js:0\\le j<m\\}$ contains $1$, $A$, $s=A^0s$ and $t=A^{m-1}s$ and is closed under multiplication and inversion, since $sA^{j}s=A^{-j}$ for every $j$: it is a subgroup of $W$ containing $s$ and $t$, so $W_0=W$. Conjugation by the elements of $W_0$ gives $A^ksA^{-k}=A^{2k}s$, $A^ktA^{-k}=A^{2k-1}s$ and $(A^ks)t(A^ks)^{-1}=A^{2k-2}s$, so every element of $T$ lies in $\\{A^js:0\\le j<m\\}$, while conversely $A^{2k}s=A^ksA^{-k}$ and $A^{2k-1}s=A^ktA^{-k}$ are conjugates of $s$ and $t$; hence $T=\\{A^js:0\\le j<m\\}$, and these $m$ elements are distinct because $A$ has order $m$ by [F1]. By the bijection $\\{\\pm\\alpha:\\alpha\\in\\Phi\\}\\to T$ of [F5] the plane has exactly $m$ root lines, and since a plane contains infinitely many lines there is a line that is none of them.",
          "step": "2.3",
          "inputs": [
            "step 1.2",
            "F1",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "Part (ii), second half. If $L=\\mathbb R\\beta$ for a root $\\beta\\in\\Phi$, then by [F5] the reflection $\\mathrm{id}-2\\Pi_L$ with normal $\\beta$ equals $r_\\beta=\\rho(t_\\beta)\\in\\rho(W)$; by step 2.2 this operator is $A_L$, so $A_L\\in\\rho(W)$. Conversely suppose $A_L\\in\\rho(W)$, say $A_L=\\rho(w)$; then $\\dim M(w)=\\dim M(A_L)=1$ by step 2.2, so the nonzero moved space $M(w)=L$ contains a root $\\beta$ by [F5], and $L=\\mathbb R\\beta$ is a root line. Hence $A_L$ lies in $\\rho(W)$ exactly when $L$ is one of the $m$ root lines, and for every other line $L$ the operator $A_L$ is an orthogonal reflection with moved space $L$ such that $A_L\\notin\\rho(W)$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "step 2.2",
            "F5"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "Collecting the verified claims: (i) is steps 1.1 and 1.2; (ii) is steps 2.2, 2.3 and 3.1; (iii) is step 2.1. In particular the converse implication $M(\\alpha)\\subseteq M(\\beta)\\Rightarrow\\alpha\\le_T\\beta$ of the rigidity theorem fails in $W=I_2(4)$ when no common upper bound is available: $\\alpha:=w_0$ and $\\beta:=A$ satisfy $M(\\alpha)=V=M(\\beta)$ by step 1.2, while $w_0\\not\\le_TA$ and $A$ and $w_0$ have no common upper bound in $W$, both by step 2.1.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
